#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114982 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "c0c08150-98f9-5677-a7a8-390cffc0c5b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4511" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\det(A) =0$\n2. The system $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution\n3. $A^{-1}$ does not exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1. $A$ is invertible.\n2. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$.\n3. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n4. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n5. $\\det(A) \\neq 0$.\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114983 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "8c1e5afa-0afa-56ee-bbfa-4e84c64093ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-4512" :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\det(A) = 0$\n2. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n3. The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1. $A$ is invertible.\n2. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$.\n3. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n4. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n5. $\\det(A) \\neq 0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n$det(A)|= det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{bmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot k - (-2) \\cdot (-2); = k - 4$,\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4$. So, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$.\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114984 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/id #uuid "cb19dcff-bb9d-55dc-941b-48153ca62e7a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4513" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n> *If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$.*\n\n1. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n2. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n3. $\\det(A) = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1. $A$ is invertible.\n2. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$.\n3. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n4. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n5. $\\det(A) \\neq 0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d75a6535-30ee-5de1-8e26-03885f172457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113319} {:answer/id #uuid "e033be82-e624-565b-affe-e0bce71492c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113320} {:answer/id #uuid "26b8abfc-eafa-5bf2-b6e4-c4ee38f1fb5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113321} {:answer/id #uuid "5bcb6422-6a32-5be8-b2e2-98536f25e72b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ is invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113322} {:answer/id #uuid "6756b341-7ed1-5a1e-b4fd-b217f3fee6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is solution to $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}x = 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bcb6422-6a32-5be8-b2e2-98536f25e72b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ is invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113322} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca572ff7-54e7-5406-8299-ff0c04d81157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113318}] :question/id #uuid "f256f6bd-b552-59e7-9a79-ba8eaef62652" :question/math-academy-id "q-31921" :question/problem "Consider the lines $2x + y = 2$ and $x + ky = 5$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n> *If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\mid __________$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca8b778a-3b92-5c07-8af0-7d29fc78a8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113373} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fe18df-a1ae-52a8-bfc7-1bdd9132a9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113374} {:answer/id #uuid "1161c7d0-938d-5b24-8896-746e88d3b5bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113375} {:answer/id #uuid "45ef9a3f-382b-5b41-b5a8-7253dbaac42b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113376} {:answer/id #uuid "70b6db37-5082-501c-9a2a-2a1f85c62a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45ef9a3f-382b-5b41-b5a8-7253dbaac42b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113376} :answer-field/id #uuid "959a3ca2-a8e8-5aad-ae3a-192c68541afc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113372}] :question/id #uuid "44e70f28-a4b9-56ce-989d-752d9ef0f612" :question/math-academy-id "q-32088" :question/problem "Let\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $det(A) = 0$\n2. $Ax = b$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\cdot 1$ column vector $b$\n3. $A^{-1}$ does **not** exist"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a66e2ad3-9381-528b-8d62-fef833a90543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113704} {:answer/id #uuid "499006b0-f8ab-55ca-becd-4d4b299aacca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113705} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc83cc9-86c9-581c-971d-0e0a533005c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113706} {:answer/id #uuid "79320fee-2d9b-5b07-881c-392096f745eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113707} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd90075-27ee-52c3-a192-1879fc3137c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 1759